  "beats": [
    {
      "beat_id": "B00",
      "act": "ASK",
      "narration_text": "Hi, I'm Sanjana, a project manager at Humanitarians AI. Here's the question I put to Claude: why is a YouTube playlist a product-strategy problem, not a filing problem? And how do I use AI as a co-strategist to fix it?",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type-on",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "greeting": "Hi, Sanjana",
            "topic": "HUMANITARIANS AI · PRODUCT STRATEGY",
            "segment": "Playlist Architecture as Product Strategy",
            "command": "Treat my YouTube playlists as products, not folders. Cluster my videos by viewer intent, map the audience journey, and tell me which playlists I'm missing.",
            "runningText": "thinking like a co-strategist…",
            "folderLabel": "@HumanitariansAI",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High",
            "output": [
              "playlists are products, not folders",
              "cluster by intent — the gaps are the weak spots",
              "same library can feed two different products"
            ]
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B00.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 15,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B00.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.85,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B00.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "EXEC-SUMMARY",
      "narration_text": "Here's the whole idea in one breath. Most channels sort playlists like folders, by topic. But a playlist is really a small product. It has an audience, an entry point, and a job to do. Treat it that way, and AI becomes your co-strategist for building it.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistExecSummary",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": "The whole idea, in one breath."
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 16,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 16.49,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "HOOK",
      "narration_text": "So let's start with the mistake. Most channels treat playlists like folders. That's it. That's the mistake. A folder is just where you file something. A playlist is a promise about what someone watches next.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistHook",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": "Start with the mistake."
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B02.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 13,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 12.86,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "REFRAME",
      "narration_text": "A folder answers where does this video live. A playlist answers what does the viewer want to watch next. Right now our channel files by project. Mycroft, NeuroVEP, RAMAN. That's the folder instinct. But every playlist should be a mini product, with its own audience, its own entry point, its own success metric. Organize by viewer intent instead of by project, and you're doing product.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistReframe",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": "Filing vs. product."
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B03.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 20,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 23.55,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "FRAMEWORK",
      "narration_text": "Every playlist has to answer three questions. One: who is it for, a beginner, a practitioner, or a decision-maker? Two: what does the viewer want after video one, reinforcement, contrast, depth, or application? Three: what should they do at the end, subscribe, watch another playlist, or act offline? If a playlist can't answer all three, it's not a product. It's a pile.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistThreeQuestions",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": "The framework: three questions."
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B04.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 23.04,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "HEADER",
      "narration_text": "Here's where AI comes in as the co-strategist. Three ways I use it every single week.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistAIHeader",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": ""
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B05.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 7,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 5.55,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "act": "METHOD-1",
      "narration_text": "One: clustering. I pasted our real playlists into Claude and asked it to group them by viewer intent, not by project. React, Lyrical Literacy and Claude cluster for beginners. Mycroft, NeuroVEP and RAMAN for practitioners. Fellows, Madison and Medhavy for decision-makers. Where those clusters disagree with our current playlists, that's where our architecture is weak.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistClustering",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": "Method 1 — cluster by intent."
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B06.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 18,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 23.59,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "act": "METHOD-2",
      "narration_text": "Two: journey mapping. For any playlist, ask AI to write the viewer's inner monologue, between video one and two, then two and three. If the transition feels forced in the text, it will feel forced on the platform too.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistJourneyMap",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": "Method 2 — map the inner monologue."
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B07.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 18,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.74,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "act": "METHOD-3",
      "narration_text": "Three: gap analysis. Ask AI to name the playlists your channel should have, but doesn't. Our HAI playlist alone is a hundred and forty-one videos: tools we shipped, learning topics, and one-off explainers, all in one pile. Hiding inside it are three real playlists. HAI Tools That Shipped for decision-makers, Learn AI for Good for beginners, and AI Explained for the curious.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistGapAnalysis",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": "Method 3 — name the missing playlists."
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B08.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 18,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 24.47,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "act": "WORKED-EXAMPLE",
      "narration_text": "Here's the payoff. Watch that one pile split. The same hundred and forty-one videos sort into three real products: the tools we built and shipped, the learning topics, and the one-off explainers. Same library, three different playlists, each for a different viewer. That's the whole move. Stop filing, start shipping products.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistTwoProducts",
          "props": {
            "sparkLine": "One pile, three products.",
            "captionLead": "One 141-video pile.",
            "captionAccent": "Three real products.",
            "containers": [
              {
                "title": "HAI Tools That Shipped",
                "accent": true
              },
              {
                "title": "Learning Topics"
              },
              {
                "title": "Explainer Videos"
              }
            ]
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B09.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 22,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 19.78,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-23T17:51:30"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "act": "TAKEAWAY",
      "narration_text": "So here's the takeaway. Playlists are the second most important product decision on your channel, right after the videos themselves. Treat them that way, and use AI to pressure-test whether your architecture actually serves a viewer, or just serves your own filing instincts.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "illustrate",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "PlaylistClosing",
          "props": {
